When I wrote Fortran programs, I rarely used a debug.  About the
only time it was necessary was when something really crazy was
taking place.  In addition, one time I was dividing two 16 bit integer
values and the Fortran compiler was doing an inline divide.  The
first step was to place the quotient into R1 and "SXT   R0".
Unfortuately, I required an UNSIGNED divide and near the end
of 1999, the quotient became negative due to Y2K issues.  After
I realized what was causing the problem, the trivial solution was
to patch the SAV file and SXT  R0  became  CLR R0.

The reason I am asking about debug utilities such as SDHX.SYS
and VDT is that they each have advantages and disadvantages,
SDHX.SYS has superb displays of the registers and the instructions
being executed and does NOT need to be LINKed into the SAV
file - but it completely freezes EVERYTHING!!!!!!!!   VDT has
very poor display features along with being LINKed into the SAV
file, but allows other programs to continue to execute.

I don't understand why DEC never managed to combine all of the
good features into one package.  It should be possible and I was
wondering if anyone else might be interested as well.

Jerome Fine

