When I wrote Fortran programs, I rarely used a debug.&nbsp; About the<br>
only time it was necessary was when something really crazy was<br>
taking place.&nbsp; In addition, one time I was dividing two 16 bit integer<br>
values and the Fortran compiler was doing an inline divide.&nbsp; The<br>
first step was to place the quotient into R1 and "SXT&nbsp;&nbsp; R0".<br>
Unfortuately, I required an UNSIGNED divide and near the end<br>
of 1999, the quotient became negative due to Y2K issues.&nbsp; After<br>
I realized what was causing the problem, the trivial solution was<br>
to patch the SAV file and SXT&nbsp; R0&nbsp; became&nbsp; CLR R0.<br>
<br>
The reason I am asking about debug utilities such as SDHX.SYS<br>
and VDT is that they each have advantages and disadvantages,<br>
SDHX.SYS has superb displays of the registers and the instructions<br>
being executed and does NOT need to be LINKed into the SAV<br>
file - but it completely freezes EVERYTHING!!!!!!!!&nbsp;&nbsp; VDT has<br>
very poor display features along with being LINKed into the SAV<br>
file, but allows other programs to continue to execute.<br>
<br>
I don't understand why DEC never managed to combine all of the<br>
good features into one package.&nbsp; It should be possible and I was<br>
wondering if anyone else might be interested as well.<br>
<br>
Jerome Fine<br>
