Yes!!  The focus of your question seems a bit clearer.  I never
mentioned the symbolic debugging aspect available in the SD:
variants under RT-11.  For many users, that would be a great
feature.  Unfortunately, it was not available under ODT under
RT-11 when I first used ODT, so I never made any use of
symbolic debugging after SD: arrived.  My starting point
in debugging is always with the MACRO-11 listing.  Once
I locate an instruction which is near the bug, setting a
breakpoint is easy and sufficient to start the process.

I find that displaying the next instruction helps far more than
anything else to verify the details associated with the MACRO-11
listing.  SD: also lists instructions at any other address along with
many other display options including an optional selection of the
automatic update of all registers at the top of the screen display.

However, the ability of VDT to support the same breakpoint
capability as SD: without freezing any thing else under RT-11,
including other jobs, makes the use of VDT much more helpful
than SD: in many cases.

What I seem to have missed is why you suggest that "the symbolic
debugger .... was the thing that you asked for" since this is the very
first time in our exchange that the features associated with a
"symbolic debugger" may or may not have been included in
the SD: variants from DEC.  While I agree that having the features
associated with a symbolic debugger are extremely useful and
very helpful in many situations, freezing all activity on the PDP-11
when a program is stopped at a breakpoint is the major aspect
and disadvantage of SD: that I would like to overcome.  It is just
as much of a disadvantage as every other feature that SD: supports
(vs what is available at present in VDT) is an advantage including,
for myself, support for a symbolic debugger within SD: (since I
don't use the symbolic debugger features in any case).

Since all of the features supported by both SD: and VDT obviously
came from DEC in the first place, the only question is which features
are supported by each of these software programs.  The point I am
attempting to make is not that DEC did not make certain features
available at all, but that it never did combine those features in one
package, in particular the ability of VDT to allow all other activity
to continue while also including all or at least most of the other
features which SD: supports.

Have I managed to make my preference clear at this point or
do you still have a question.  I do appreciate the opportunity
to discuss these points since it seems that very few individuals
are interested.  This discussion also helps me to clarify what
I specifically want in an enhanced SD: and may point to other
avenues to find a solution.  THANK  YOU!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!

Jerome Fine


