On Mon, 27 May 2013 10:05:39 -0400, "Jerome H. Fine"
<everyone@nospam.com> wrote:

>Does anyone reading this still write code to be run
>under RT-11?  When a program has a bug, what do
>you use to find the problem?  For debugging programs,
>SD: seems the most efficient way, especially when a
>Mapped Monitor is being used.  Alternatively, ODT
>(VDT when Multi-terminal Support is included in the
>RT-11 monitor) can be LINKed into the program.
>
>Recently, I have been considering enhancements to
>the SD: variant, SDHX.SYS, which would reduce
>the Low Memory required along with adding an
>additional feature which is part of Ersatz-11.
>Specifically, SET  PCLOG  ON requests the
>Ersatz-11 emulator to save the addresses of the
>last 64 instructions that were executed.  The the
>command SHOW  PCLOG displays all 64 addresses
>at the users request.
>
>I would appreciate a response from anyone still writing
>and debugging programs that run under RT-11 and
>which debug package is being used along with the
>advantages and disadvantages that have been found.

I still write and debug RT-11 programs, but mostly running under RUST,
or more recently under RTX (RUST running under RSX). I use my own
debuggers. For MACRO, I use output logging and a single breakpoint, or
wait for a memory or instruction error to trigger the debugger. I
haven't used a symbolic debugger for a long time--I have the program
source available on a different screen and read from that. Every so
often I need the link map to get a symbolic address. For C programs I
use printf almost exclusively, although my preprocessor has a feature
which allows me to log progress at the terminal very easily.

Most of my debugging is done under the V11 emulator which has a great
debugger where I can trap all kinds of behaviors. Along with WATCH it
also supports FEEL which traps when a particular location is read. A
couple of years ago I wrote a new debugger for RUST/SJ with full trace
trap support. RUST/XM has yet another debugger which runs in kernel
space--it allows ordinary system usage to continue while debugging in
user or kernel mode (although you have to be careful).

V11 and the new RUST/SJ debugger support a command history. The L
(last) and N (next) debug commands walk through the command history
(like your PCLOG).

