No.

> Is the debugger able to WATCH a location and trigger the debugger when
> the location changes?

Partly indirectly. You can have breakpoints, which only stops the 
program if a value have changed, for instance. But not generic watchpoints.

The syntax for setting a breakpoint is:
"SET BREAK <address> WHEN <expression> DO <action>"

There are some additional things you can do as well, but essentially it 
means you can look for changes, but you can't make it happen 
(reasonably) after each instruction.

My modified decus debugger do support watchpoints, tracepoints, history, 
profiling and some other nifty stuff. However, it does not handle 
overlays any better than plain ODT.

